Issue #240 — the caller half: a silent review's stale verdict labels come off
before anything fallible is attempted, and a notification that fails no longer
takes the invalidation down with it.

WHY THIS IS A FILE AND NOT A COMMIT. Agents here run as `claude[bot]` through
the Claude GitHub App, `ATTADIPA_AGENT_TOKEN` is unset by design, and that
installation token holds no `workflows` permission. Verified on 2026-08-25 by
pushing this branch with the workflow edit in it:

    ! [remote rejected] fix/review-invalidation-order-240 -> ...
      (refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow
       `.github/workflows/claude-pr-review.yml` without `workflows` permission)

So `.github/scripts/review-invalidate.sh` — which is the whole of the fix — is
on `main` with its behaviour asserted on every push, and the two steps that CALL
it are here. Until this lands, the live `claude-pr-review.yml` keeps the order
the issue describes: a silent review whose `gh pr comment` fails leaves the
previous head's `ai-review:pass` on the pull request.

ONE FILE, AND THAT IS A CONSTRAINT RATHER THAN A CHOICE. This patch carries
`claude-pr-review.yml` and nothing else. It wanted a second hunk — a line in
`ci.yml` for `.github/tests/review-invalidate-workflow-test.sh` — and it may not
have one: `75-approval-stall.patch` already carries `ci.yml`, and
`gh-api-usage-test.sh` refuses two parked patches carrying one workflow file,
because landing either would leave the other's copy stale and that is fatal on
`main` and on every open pull request. So the test runs from inside
`review-published-test.sh` instead, on the `ci.yml` line that is already there,
and it reads the patched workflow out of THIS FILE while this file is parked —
which is strictly better than a dormant test: the fix below is executed against
a stub `gh` on every push, today, and the day it lands the test switches to the
real workflow without an edit.

WHAT IT CHANGES:

  - `Say that the review published nothing` delegates to the helper and exits
    with the helper's status. Its own `set -euo pipefail` becomes `set -uo
    pipefail`, which is the fix rather than a relaxation: the helper decides
    what is fatal, and only the invalidation is.
  - It gains `TRUSTED: ${{ env.ATTADIPA_TRUSTED_SCRIPTS }}` in `env:`, the same
    way the convergence step below it already takes it, and a two-line fallback
    for a bundle that could not be staged. That fallback is unreachable in
    practice — without the bundle the step above cannot run `review-published.sh`
    and never sets `state=silent` — and it is there because a missing helper
    exiting 0 would rebuild the exact hole this closes.
  - `Say that the review did not happen` hoists its `--remove-label
    ai-review:pass` above the comment body, the dedupe read and the post, and
    drops the `|| true` that made a genuinely failed removal report success.
    Same defect, same file, three lines. `ai-review:blocking` is still not
    removed there, deliberately: a review that did not run has said nothing
    that justifies releasing a hold.

TO APPLY, from a session whose token may write workflows:

    git checkout main && git pull
    git apply docs/automation/pending/240-review-invalidation-order.patch
    git rm docs/automation/pending/240-review-invalidation-order.patch
    #  This file only. The directory also holds `75-approval-stall.patch` and
    #  `170-merge-sweep-completeness.patch`, both still parked, plus the README
    #  that three links in APPROVAL_STALLS.md point at.
    bash .github/tests/review-published-test.sh   # 54 assertions, and the last
                                                  # 25 flip to the applied state
    actionlint .github/workflows/claude-pr-review.yml
    python3 tools/docs/check_docs.py .

THEN, IN THE SAME COMMIT, the prose that describes the parked state. There is no
hunk for it below and that is deliberate: this directory's README says the
*Waiting now* table has to be a named instruction rather than a diff, because a
hunk against that table breaks the moment anybody parks another patch. The other
three are here for the same reason — a hunk against a paragraph is a hunk that
rots when somebody edits the sentence beside it.

